Scope
These are lecture notes for Quantum Information and Computing (third-semester course, Master in Physics of Data, University of Padova; lectures by Prof. Giuseppe Vallone). The course has three modules:

Fundamentals of quantum mechanics — Hilbert spaces, pure and mixed states, measurements and POVMs, unitary evolution, composite systems, entanglement, Bell inequalities;
Quantum information — classical and quantum Shannon theory, no-cloning, quantum key distribution, teleportation and entanglement swapping;
Quantum computation — classical and quantum circuits, universal gates, the quantum Fourier transform, phase estimation, Shor’s and Grover’s algorithms, error correction and physical implementations.

A scalar (inner) product assigns to each pair of vectors a complex number \(\braket{\phi|\psi}\) such that:

\(\braket{\phi|\psi} = \braket{\psi|\phi}^*\) (conjugate symmetry);
it is linear in the second argument: \(\braket{\phi|\,a\psi_1 + b\psi_2} = a\braket{\phi|\psi_1} + b\braket{\phi|\psi_2}\);
\(\braket{\psi|\psi} \geq 0\), with equality iff \(\ket\psi = 0\).

In an orthonormal basis, \(\braket{\phi|\psi} = \sum_k \phi_k^* \psi_k\). The norm is \(\|\psi\| = \sqrt{\braket{\psi|\psi}}\).



The bra \(\bra\phi\) is the linear functional \(\ket\psi \mapsto \braket{\phi|\psi}\). It is the Hermitian conjugate of the ket: as a row vector, \(\bra\phi = (\ket\phi)^\dagger = (\phi_0^*, \dots, \phi_{d-1}^*)\). Conjugation is antilinear: \((a\ket\phi)^\dagger = a^* \bra\phi\).

The density operator of the ensemble \(\{p_k, \ket{\psi_k}\}\) is \[\hat\rho = \sum_k p_k\,\ket{\psi_k}\bra{\psi_k}.\] A state is pure if \(\hat\rho = \ket\psi\bra\psi\) for some \(\ket\psi\), and mixed otherwise.


An operator \(\hat\rho\) is a density operator of some ensemble iff it is

Hermitian, \(\hat\rho^\dagger = \hat\rho\);
positive semidefinite, \(\braket{\phi|\hat\rho|\phi} \geq 0\) for all \(\ket\phi\);
of unit trace, \(\mathrm{Tr}\,\hat\rho = 1\).




For a state \(\hat\rho\): \[p_a = \mathrm{Tr}[\hat\Pi_a\hat\rho], \qquad \langle\hat A\rangle = \mathrm{Tr}[\hat A\hat\rho], \qquad \hat\rho \ \xrightarrow{\ a\ }\ \frac{\hat\Pi_a\hat\rho\,\hat\Pi_a}{p_a}.\]

\(\hat U\) is unitary if \(\hat U^\dagger\hat U = \hat U\hat U^\dagger = \hat{\mathbb 1}\). It preserves scalar products, \(\braket{\hat U\phi|\hat U\psi} = \braket{\phi|\psi}\), hence norms and orthogonality; it maps orthonormal bases to orthonormal bases; its eigenvalues have unit modulus, \(e^{i\lambda}\).








Three kinds of operators play very different roles and should not be confused:

observables \(\hat A = \hat A^\dagger\) label measurement outcomes; applying \(\hat A\) to a state, \(\hat A\ket\psi\), has no physical meaning (it is not even normalised);
density operators \(\hat\rho \geq 0\), \(\mathrm{Tr}\hat\rho = 1\) describe states;
unitaries \(\hat U^\dagger\hat U = \hat{\mathbb 1}\) describe transformations.

The Pauli matrices are special: they are both Hermitian and unitary, so \(\hat\sigma_x\) is at the same time “spin along \(x\)” (an observable) and “bit flip” (a transformation). Projectors \(\hat\Pi_a\) appear inside measurements, but “\(\hat\Pi_a\ket\psi\)” is only the unnormalised post-measurement state.

when both check midway, both cakes have risen early \(9\%\) of the time;
whenever Alice’s cake rose early, Bob’s tasted good;
whenever Bob’s cake rose early, Alice’s tasted good.



Encode \(\ket G = \ket 0\), \(\ket B = \ket 1\) and the midway test in the rotated basis \[\ket R = \sqrt{\tfrac25}\ket 0 + \sqrt{\tfrac35}\ket 1, \qquad \ket N = \sqrt{\tfrac35}\ket 0 - \sqrt{\tfrac25}\ket 1,\] and prepare \[\ket\psi = \frac12\ket{11} - \sqrt{\tfrac38}\bigl(\ket{01} + \ket{10}\bigr).\] Then \(\braket{GG|\psi} = 0\), so \(P(G_A, G_B) = 0\); \(\braket{R B|\psi} = \tfrac12\sqrt{\tfrac35} - \sqrt{\tfrac38}\sqrt{\tfrac25} = 0\), so a risen cake on one side implies a good one on the other; and \(\braket{RR|\psi} = \tfrac12\cdot\tfrac35 - 2\sqrt{\tfrac38}\cdot\tfrac{\sqrt6}{5} = -0.3\), so \(P(R_A, R_B) = 0.09\).

For a random variable \(X\) with distribution \(p_x\), \[H(X) = -\sum_x p_x\log_2 p_x .\]




joint entropy \(H(X, Y) = -\sum_{x,y}p_{xy}\log_2 p_{xy}\);
conditional entropy \(H(X|Y) = H(X,Y) - H(Y)\): the additional bits needed to specify \(X\) when \(Y\) is known — the residual ignorance about \(X\);
mutual information \(I(X:Y) = H(X) - H(X|Y) = H(Y) - H(Y|X) = H(X) + H(Y) - H(X,Y)\): how much learning \(Y\) reduces the bits needed to specify \(X\).




A channel defined by conditional probabilities \(p(y|x)\) has capacity \[C = \max_{p_x} I(X:Y).\] Every rate \(R &lt; C\) can be transmitted with vanishing error probability, and no rate \(R &gt; C\) can.

P: problems solvable by a deterministic algorithm in time polynomial in \(n\) — “efficiently solvable”;
NP: problems whose solutions can be verified in polynomial time (factoring, satisfiability, travelling salesman decision version); whether \(\mathrm P = \mathrm{NP}\) is open;
BPP: solvable in polynomial time by a randomised algorithm with error probability \(\leq \tfrac13\);
PSPACE: solvable with polynomial memory;
BQP: solvable in polynomial time by a quantum computer with bounded error.
